      "left": "the empennage",
      "right": "stability and fine control — vertical fin plus rudder, horizontal stabiliser plus elevator"
    },
    {
      "left": "flaps",
      "right": "trade drag for lift at low speed — the controllable approach"
    },
    {
      "left": "trim tabs",
      "right": "neutralise control pressure so the aircraft flies hands-off"
    }
  ],
  "why": "The whole system is rigged so stability, not the pilot, is the first line of level flight — disturb a stable aircraft and it seeks to return itself."
};

const asiMixtureSpot = {
  "prompt": "This engine briefing contains one faulty line — click it:",
  "lines": [
    "Most trainers burn 100LL avgas in an air-cooled four-cylinder boxer engine turning a propeller at around 2,400 rpm.",
    "The cycle is the familiar four-stroke: intake, compression, power, exhaust.",
    "The mixture must be leaned in flight because air density falls with altitude.",
    "Too rich a mixture burns pistons; too lean wastes fuel and fouls plugs.",
    "On the ground, a hot day or high field elevation saps takeoff power the same way."
  ],
  "errorLine": 3,
  "correction": "Inverted: too RICH wastes fuel and fouls plugs; too LEAN burns pistons — the lean side is the dangerous one.",
  "why": "The mixture knob is a power-and-safety control: the engine breathes thinner air up high, so the fuel–air ratio follows it."
};

const asiPropCompare = {
  "prompt": "Two prop philosophies — drag between them:",
  "before": {
    "label": "Fixed-pitch prop",
    "text": "A compromise blade, best at one regime — the trainer's fitting: simple, rugged, adequate."
  },
  "after": {
    "label": "Constant-speed prop",
    "text": "Blade angle varies to keep the engine efficient across the whole envelope — the upgrade that separates trainers from touring aircraft."
  },
  "why": "The propeller is itself a rotating wing — so the prop choice is really a mission choice."
};

const asiRiskPredict = {
  "q": "Which system causes most light-aircraft accidents?",
  "choices": [
    "the engine — mechanical failure dominates accident statistics",
    "decisions: fuel exhaustion, weather continued-into, manoeuvring low over the ground",
    "the landing gear collapsing on touchdown"
  ],
  "answer": 1,
  "why": "Modern aero engines are astonishingly reliable — most accidents are not mechanical at all. Systems knowledge is really decision knowledge: fuel selection, mixture, oil pressure are the pilot's to manage."
};

const asiPanelBuckets = {
  "prompt": "The six-pack divides by what each dial trusts — file them:",
  "buckets": [
    "Pitot-static",
    "Gyroscopic",
    "Neither — direct reading"
  ],
  "items": [
    {
      "label": "airspeed indicator — ram air minus static pressure",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "altimeter — an aneroid barometer calibrated to altitude",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "vertical speed indicator — how fast pressure is changing",
      "bucket": 0
    },
    {
      "label": "attitude indicator — rigidity in space",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "heading indicator — realigned to the compass every so often",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "turn coordinator — rate and quality of turn",
      "bucket": 1
    },
    {
      "label": "the magnetic compass — needs no power at all",
      "bucket": 2
    }
  ],
  "why": "Two pressure sensors feed three instruments; spinning gyros feed three more — knowing what each dial trusts is what lets you reason about what a stuck instrument will read."
};

const asiGyroPlace = {
  "prompt": "The gyroscopic heading indicator slowly precesses. About how often must it be realigned to the magnetic compass?",
  "labels": [
    "0",
    "15",
    "30",
    "45",
    "60"
  ],
  "scale": {
    "min": 0,
    "max": 60
  },
  "answer": 15,
  "tolerance": 3,
  "why": "About every fifteen minutes — the gyro holds a steady reference the compass cannot, but precession creeps in, so the compass periodically re-truths it.",
  "context": "The compass swings in turns and accelerations; the gyro drifts in between — which is why the two are cross-checked rather than trusted alone."
};

const asiFailJudge = {
  "statements": [
    {
      "s": "Instruments fail politely and wrongly — a slowly winding-down gyro, an airspeed indicator drifting upward.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The dangerous moment is never the failure itself but the pilot flying by an instrument that has quietly begun to lie."
    },
    {
      "s": "Instruments fail loudly, with a warning light for every fault.",
      "truth": false,
      "why": "Some failures are engineered to indicate (low-vacuum, low-voltage), but the design assumes the residual failure will be silent."
    },
    {
      "s": "The defence is the cross-check: instruments are read as a set, and any single dissenter is suspect.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "Each confirming the others — which is why the training is so relentless."
    },
    {
      "s": "A pilot without outside references who trusts a lying instrument can enter a graveyard spiral within a minute.",
      "truth": true,
      "why": "The attitude feels level, the circle tightens, the descent steepens — spatial disorientation is what instrument flying was invented to defeat."
    }
  ]
};

const asiWalkOrder = {
  "prompt": "The preflight that catches problems before they fly — put it in order:",
  "items": [
    "in the cabin: documents (ARROW), controls and switches, fuel selector",
    "fuel samples drained from sumps at each tank and the gascolator",
    "oil quantity and security; propeller and spinner inspected for nicks",
    "control surfaces moved through full travel — full and free",
    "tyres, brakes, shock struts, antennas — and the pitot tube uncovered"
  ],
  "why": "The fuel sample is the single most important check — water sinks below the gasoline in the cup — and a pitot cover forgotten on the tube has quietly produced airspeed-zero takeoffs."
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/aviation/foundational/aircraft-systems-and-instruments">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is the beginner lesson of Aviation. You need zero prior knowledge — no flying
    experience, no physics, no jargon. We start with a walk around a small training
    aircraft, and learn the machine before the flying.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="drag the divider — fixed versus constant-speed" widget="compare" config={asiPropCompare} />
  <p>By the end of this lesson you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>name the airframe's main parts and say which control surface moves the aircraft about which axis;</li>
    <li>explain where a trainer's engine gets its spark, and why the mixture knob exists;</li>
    <li>trace the pitot-static system: two sensors, three instruments, and how each fails;</li>
    <li>explain why gyroscopic instruments need cross-checking, and why instruments fail politely;</li>
    <li>run the preflight walkaround's logic — why each check exists.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Every new term is explained the moment it appears. Take your time — one idea per
    paragraph.
  </p>

  <h2>Start with a machine you can see</h2>
  <p>
    Open the door of a small training aircraft and you can see almost everything. There
    are no hidden compartments here: an airframe of aluminium and rivets, an engine
    descended from a 1940s car motor, six round dials on the panel fed by two pressure
    sensors and two spinning gyros. A light aircraft is a wonderfully inspectable system,
    flown by a single human whose first professional job is understanding what each part
    does — and, more importantly, how each part fails.
  </p>
  <p>
    The design philosophy underneath is redundancy and honesty. Systems are duplicated
    (two ignition systems, multiple fuel pumps, backup static sources), and the pilot is
    trained not just in operation but in failure modes. The dangerous moment is never the
    failure itself but the pilot flying the aircraft by an instrument that has quietly
    begun to lie.
  </p>
  <p>
    Everything in the private-pilot knowledge syllabus hangs from that competence. The
    airframe chapter explains what each surface does to each axis; the powerplant chapter
    explains where the thrust comes from and why the mixture knob exists; the instruments
    chapter explains what each dial trusts — which sensor, which principle — so that a
    pilot can reason about what a stuck instrument will read; and the preflight inspection
    turns the whole body of knowledge into a ten-minute walk with a fuel cup. This lesson
    takes those four subjects in exactly that order.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the aircraft as a system, part by part" widget="equationStep" config={systemsCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> press "Walk me through it" and reveal the five system
    groups in the order this lesson takes them — the airframe and its three control axes, the
    engine and propeller, the pitot-static sensors, the gyros, and the preflight inspection.
    Each step's note already points at the failure modes to come: three inputs for three
    axes, two sensors feeding three instruments, and a walkaround that catches problems
    before they fly.
  </p>

  <h2>Airframe, engine and propeller</h2>
  <p>
    The airframe divides into three functional parts. The <em>fuselage</em> carries crew,
    payload and the tail. The <em>wing</em> makes lift and houses the main structural
    members (spars carrying bending loads, ribs holding shape, stressed skin sharing the
    work — the <em>semi-monocoque</em> construction that makes light aircraft both light
    and strong). The <em>empennage</em> — the tail assembly — provides the stability and
    fine control: vertical fin plus movable rudder, horizontal stabiliser plus movable
    elevator.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="pair each part with its job" widget="match" config={asiAirframeMatch} />
  <p>
    The control system is direct and mechanical: ailerons in the wing trailing edges for
    roll, the elevator for pitch, the rudder for yaw — three inputs, three axes
    (longitudinal, lateral, vertical, all passing through the centre of gravity),
    connected by cables and pushrods a pilot can inspect. Flaps extend from the wings to
    trade drag for lift at low speed — the difference between a controllable approach and
    an unlandable aeroplane. Trim tabs neutralise control pressure so the aircraft can be
    flown hands-off. The whole system is rigged so that <em>stability</em>, not the pilot,
    is the first line of level flight: disturb a stable aircraft and it seeks to return
    itself, which is why hands can leave the yoke for a chart.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> the rudder is what turns the aircraft — that a plane
  steers like a boat or a car, with the rudder pedalling the nose around. In normal flight
  the turn is made with the <em>ailerons</em>: banking the wing tilts the lift vector
  sideways, and it is that tilted lift that curves the flight path, with the elevator
  holding the pitch and the rudder's job being to coordinate — damping adverse yaw, not
  doing the steering. An instructor's phrase for it: the rudder points the nose; the
  ailerons turn the aeroplane.</p>
  <p>
    The engine is the next surprise. Most trainers burn aviation gasoline (100LL avgas) in
    an air-cooled, four-cylinder <em>boxer</em> engine — cylinders lying flat opposite
    each other, shaking each other balanced — turning a propeller at around 2,400 rpm. The
    cycle is the familiar four-stroke one (intake, compression, power, exhaust), but two
    aviation-specific systems deserve attention.
  </p>
  <p>
    First, ignition: the spark comes from <em>magnetos</em> — engine-driven generators
    needing no battery or aircraft electrical system at all. That is why an electrical
    failure does not stop the engine, and why the preflight check includes killing the
    electrical master and confirming the engine keeps running. Second, the
    <em>mixture</em> control: the engine breathes air whose density falls with altitude,
    so the fuel–air ratio must be leaned in flight (too rich wastes fuel and fouls plugs;
    too lean burns pistons), and on the ground a hot day or high field elevation saps
    takeoff power the same way.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="find the faulty line — the mixture briefing" widget="spot-error" config={asiMixtureSpot} />
  <p>
    The <em>propeller</em> is itself a rotating wing. A fixed-pitch prop is a compromise
    blade, best at one regime; constant-speed props vary their blade angle to keep the
    engine efficient across the whole envelope — the upgrade that separates trainers from
    touring aircraft.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the engine is the riskiest system in the aircraft.
    Modern aero engines are astonishingly reliable — most accidents are not mechanical
    at all, but decisions: fuel exhaustion, weather continued-into, manoeuvring
    low over the ground. The engine's failure modes are also the pilot's to manage
    (fuel selection, mixture, oil pressure monitoring), which is why systems knowledge
    is really decision knowledge.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="bet first — what actually downplanes trainers?" widget="predict" config={asiRiskPredict} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> three surfaces for three axes, stability rigged into the
    airframe, and an engine whose ignition ignores the electrics. Now the panel: what each
    dial actually listens to.
  </p>

  <h2>Flight instruments and the pitot-static system</h2>
  <p>
    The classic "six-pack" panel divides by sensor. Three instruments are
    <em>pitot-static</em> — fed by two small sensing points on the airframe. The
    <em>pitot tube</em>, facing into the wind, captures ram air: the difference between
    that impact pressure and the ambient <em>static</em> pressure (from flush vents on the
    fuselage side) is the dynamic pressure from aerodynamics, and the <em>airspeed
    indicator</em> is a pressure gauge reading exactly that. The static source alone drives
    the other two: the <em>altimeter</em> is an aneroid barometer calibrated to read
    altitude from ambient pressure, and the <em>vertical speed indicator</em> reads how
    fast that pressure is changing.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="file the instruments — which sensor feeds which dial?" widget="sort-buckets" config={asiPanelBuckets} />
  <p>
    The sensor plumbing explains the failure modes, which pilots learn as behaviour, not
    symptoms. Block the pitot tube (ice is the classic; a mud-dauber wasp the memorable
    one) and the airspeed indicator stops tracking speed: with the drain hole also blocked
    it reads like a crude altimeter — climbing as you climb — while the real hazard is
    believing it. Block the static vent and the altimeter and vertical speed freeze while
    the airspeed indicator reads erratically. Aircraft therefore carry pitot heat
    (electrically warming the tube against ice) and an alternate static source.
  </p>
  <p>
    The three <em>gyroscopic</em> instruments run on a different principle: a spinning
    rotor resists changes of orientation (rigidity in space) — like a spinning top that
    stands upright. The <em>attitude indicator</em> shows the horizon against a miniature
    aircraft. The <em>heading indicator</em> holds a steady reference the magnetic compass
    cannot (the compass swings in turns and accelerations), but slowly precesses and must
    be realigned to the compass every fifteen minutes or so. The <em>turn coordinator</em>
    shows rate and quality of turn. Historically these ran off engine-driven vacuum pumps
    — which is why "lose the vacuum pump" was a legendary instrument-flight emergency, and
    why modern panels back up air-driven gyros with electric ones.
  </p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="pin the realignment interval on the clock" widget="place" config={asiGyroPlace} />
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> instruments fail loudly. They fail politely and
    wrongly — a slowly winding-down gyro, an airspeed indicator drifting upward in the
    climb, a compass that stops agreeing with the heading indicator. The defence is
    the cross-check: instruments are read as a set, each confirming the others, and
    any single dissenter is suspect. The reason the training is so relentless is the
    alternative: a pilot without outside references who trusts a lying instrument can
    enter a graveyard spiral within a minute — the attitude feels level, the circle
    tightens, the descent steepens, and the accident investigation reads like a
    physics proof. Spatial disorientation, not darkness, is what instrument flying
    was invented to defeat.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="true or false — the polite failure" widget="judge" config={asiFailJudge} />
  <p>
    Even the honest instruments need interpretation, and the altimeter is the standing
    example: it reads altitude <em>above a pressure level</em>, not above the ground, so
    the pilot must set the local pressure setting (the "Kollsman window" on the dial) or
    everyone's altimeters disagree by tens to hundreds of feet — the reason air traffic
    control issues settings constantly, and the reason a stale setting is a written-off
    flight at low level. The magnetic compass, the one instrument needing no power at all,
    carries its own catalogue of built-in errors: <em>variation</em> (the difference
    between true and magnetic north, which changes across the map), <em>deviation</em>
    (distortion from the aircraft's own iron and electronics, corrected on the "compass
    correction card"), and acceleration and turning errors that pilots memorise by
    acronym. Every instrument, in short, is a small physical theory with stated
    assumptions — and instrument training is mostly learning, for each one, what its
    assumptions are and when the ground truth will diverge.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> two pressure sensors feed three instruments, spinning gyros
    feed three more, and every one of them can fail quietly — so pilots read them as a
    set. Last system: the walk that catches problems before they fly.
  </p>

  <h2>The preflight inspection</h2>
  <p>
    The preflight walkaround is where systems knowledge becomes safety, and it follows the
    checklist — read and done, every flight, no matter how many thousands of hours. In the
    cabin: documents (airworthiness certificate, registration, operating handbook,
    weight-and-balance data — the "ARROW" memory aid), controls and switches, fuel
    selector.
  </p>
  <p>
    Outside, working around the aircraft: <em>fuel samples drained from sumps at each tank
    and the gascolator</em> — the single most important check, since water in the fuel
    (condensation, or bad fuel) sinks below the gasoline in the sample cup and ends
    flights before they start. Then oil quantity and security; the propeller and spinner
    inspected for nicks (a nick is a stress riser in a spinning wing); control surfaces
    moved through their full travel and checked for full and <em>free</em> movement,
    hinges and attach bolts; tyres, brakes, shock struts; antennas; and the pitot tube
    uncovered — because the protective cover forgotten on the pitot has quietly produced
    airspeed-zero takeoffs. Fuel quantity is gauged, then physically verified, because
    fuel gauges are legally allowed to be approximate and the one thing they must not be
    trusted for is whether there is enough to get home.
  </p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="rebuild the walkaround, cabin to pitot" widget="order" config={asiWalkOrder} />
  <p>
    The deeper point is cultural. The walkaround is the pilot's first line of maintenance
    — the system that catches the loose bolt, the flat strut, the contaminated tank before
    they become flight issues — and it runs on the same discipline as everything else in
    aviation: written checklists, read-back, nothing from memory, nothing from habit.
    Aviation's real technology is arguably not the airframe but that culture — checklists,
    cross-checks, written standards, and the professional humility to follow them — and
    the preflight is where a student pilot first practises it. The habit generalises to
    every system on the aircraft: anything that matters is checked before it is trusted,
    and the check is written down.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. The answers are right below.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Which control surface actually turns the aircraft in normal flight, and how?</li>
    <li>Why does an electrical failure not stop a trainer's engine?</li>
    <li>The airspeed indicator reads like an altimeter, climbing as you climb. What failed, and what is the real danger?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Answers:</strong> (1) The ailerons — banking the wings tilts the lift vector
    sideways, and that tilted lift curves the flight path; the rudder only coordinates.
    (2) The spark comes from magnetos, engine-driven generators that need no battery or
    electrical system. (3) The pitot tube is blocked (both it and its drain hole); the
    danger is trusting a lying airspeed indicator — which is why instruments are
    cross-checked as a set.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Three control surfaces map to three axes — ailerons roll, elevator pitches, rudder yaws — and banking with the ailerons is what actually turns the aircraft.</li>
    <li>Magnetos supply the spark with no battery or electrical system, which is why an electrical failure does not stop the engine.</li>
    <li>The mixture knob exists because air density falls with altitude; leaning is a power-and-safety control, not a fuel-saving nicety.</li>
    <li>Two pressure sensors feed three pitot-static instruments, and blocking either sensor produces a specific, learnable lie.</li>
    <li>Gyros hold their reference but precess and fail quietly — instruments are cross-checked as a set, and any dissenter is suspect.</li>
    <li>The preflight walkaround, checklist in hand, is the pilot's first line of maintenance: fuel samples, control freedom, pitot uncovered.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    You now know the machine; the next lessons teach what it does in the air. Aerodynamics
    explains the lift that the ailerons bank and the elevator trims; from there the units move
    on to weather, navigation and regulations, and eventually to flying by instruments alone.
    Systems knowledge first is the private-pilot syllabus's own order — know what each part
    does, and how it fails, before asking it to carry you.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The system design rewards a systems-engineer's reading, because the patterns are
    textbook. <em>Redundancy</em> is layered by failure class: magnetos are duplicated
    per cylinder (and each alone runs the engine), because ignition is flight-critical;
    the vacuum system gets an electric backup because instruments are critical in cloud;
    static ports get alternates. <em>Failure indication</em> is engineered where
    possible (a low-vacuum light, a low-voltage light, fuel-pressure gauges), but the
    design assumes the residual failure will be silent — hence the cross-check culture.
    The <em>glass cockpit</em> generation replaced the six-pack with two screens, which
    changes the failure discussion without removing it: the failure modes migrate from
    plumbing to software and power, and the training answers with the same principle —
    know what each display trusts, and have a thought for what you will believe when it
    stops.
  </p>
  <p>
    The open questions are the industry's. Electric propulsion removes avgas, magnetos
    and mixture — and adds battery state-of-charge as a safety-critical quantity with
    no decades-old certification heritage. Automation keeps moving pilot tasks into
    software (autoland is now a certified feature on some airframes), reviving the
    old question of what the pilot must remain able to do unaided. And the maintenance
    side is where the future of the fleet actually lives: the average training aircraft
    is older than its instructor's parents, kept airworthy by inspection programmes
    rather than replacement — which is why the walkaround, the checklist and the
    "know your machine" commandment remain the load-bearing structure of the whole
    safety case.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> FAA Private Pilot ACS-6C and <em>Pilot's Handbook of Aeronautical Knowledge</em> (FAA-H-8083-25). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
